<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Las Salinas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1538">1538</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-wars-between-the-conquistadors-in-peru">Civil wars between the conquistadors in Peru</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Las Salinas was fought in April 1538 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> between the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a>, led by his brother Hernando, and those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-de-almagro">Diego de Almagro</a>, in the first of the civil wars among the conquistadors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. Almagro's army was routed, and he was captured, tried and executed at Cusco in July 1538.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-las-salinas.jpg" alt="Battle of Las Salinas" width="320" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diego-de-Almagro.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The quarrel between the Pizarro brothers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-de-almagro">Diego de Almagro</a> grew out of a dispute over the possession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cuzco</a> during the first Spanish partition and administration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. Both camps claimed to act for the Spanish Crown: Pizarro's forces held the province of Nueva Castilla, Almagro's that of Nueva Toledo. Almagro had held Cuzco since 1537, having taken it by a coup de main, but Pizarro's forces were by far the strongest in the region. Weakened by a debilitating disease, Almagro left the campaign to his lieutenant, Rodrigo Orgóñez.</p><p>Almagro's men failed to secure the Guaitara pass guarding the approach to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cuzco</a>, and the Pizarros' army crossed the mountains. At a war council in Cuzco Almagro still considered negotiating; Orgóñez is said to have answered that it was too late, since Almagro had freed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernando-pizarro">Hernando Pizarro</a> and nothing remained but to fight him. Orgóñez marched his 500 men to the old salt mines of Cachipampa, about 5 km south of the city.</p><p>His choice of ground has been criticised: the broken terrain hampered his cavalry, more than half his force, and many of his infantry carried only pikes, though a battery of six falconets gave him an advantage. Pizarro's army of about 700 was mostly infantry, with veteran conquistadors and a contingent of Imperial arquebusiers recently arrived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Both armies placed their infantry in the centre and cavalry on the wings, with Alonso de Alvarado and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernando-pizarro">Hernando Pizarro</a> commanding Pizarro's two cavalry corps. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonzalo-pizarro">Gonzalo Pizarro</a> led the infantry across the small river between the armies. Orgóñez's guns threw his column into disorder, but the swampy ground kept Orgóñez's horsemen from exploiting it, and the Imperial arquebusiers, once across, opened a heavy fire of double-headed shot.</p><p>With the infantry locked in combat in the marshes, each side gathered its cavalry into a single column, and the two met at full gallop, the men shouting for the king and for Almagro or for Pizarro. Orgóñez was shot, unhorsed and killed while offering to surrender, and his cavalry fell back in confusion despite its superiority. After an hour Almagro's outgunned infantry scattered towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cuzco</a>. Almagro, who had watched from a litter on a hill, rode off on a mule to the fortress of Cuzco, and the Pizarros took the city. Juan de Málaga, the husband of the conquistadora Inés Suárez, reportedly died in the battle.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Las Salinas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Almagro was defeated at the <b>Battle of Las Salinas</b> in 1538 and executed.</blockquote><a href="/state/governorate-of-new-toledo">Governorate of New Toledo</a></li></ul></section>
